<header class="ws-head"><p class="ws-kicker">Worksheet &middot; Reading path</p><h1 class="ws-title">Worksheet: Religion and Power</h1><p class="ws-lede">How rulers adopted, promoted and imposed faiths, and how faith in turn shaped their states.</p><p class="ws-who" aria-hidden="true"><span>Name</span><span>Class</span><span>Date</span></p><p class="ws-tools"><button type="button" class="ws-print" data-key="0">Print the worksheet</button><button type="button" class="ws-print" data-key="1">Print with the answers</button><a href="/paths/religion-and-power">Open the path &rarr;</a></p></header>
<section class="ws-task"><h2><span class="ws-n">1</span>Put the stops in order</h2><p class="ws-how">Number the stops from 1 (earliest) to 6 (latest), by when each began: a state&#8217;s founding, a person&#8217;s birth, the date of an event.</p><ul class="ws-order"><li><span class="ws-box" aria-hidden="true"></span><span>Rashidun Caliphate</span></li><li><span class="ws-box" aria-hidden="true"></span><span>Coronation of Charlemagne</span></li><li><span class="ws-box" aria-hidden="true"></span><span>Aksumite Empire</span></li><li><span class="ws-box" aria-hidden="true"></span><span>Edict of Milan</span></li><li><span class="ws-box" aria-hidden="true"></span><span>Safavid Empire</span></li><li><span class="ws-box" aria-hidden="true"></span><span>Maurya Empire</span></li></ul></section>
<section class="ws-task"><h2><span class="ws-n">2</span>Think and discuss</h2><ol class="ws-qs"><li><p>Stop 1, <b>Maurya Empire</b>. The path begins here because: &#8220;After the bloody Kalinga War, Ashoka renounced conquest by force, and royal patronage helped spread Buddhism across the subcontinent and beyond.&#8221; What does this stop tell you about how the story began?</p><span class="ws-lines" aria-hidden="true"><span></span><span></span><span></span></span></li><li><p>Stop 5, <b>Rashidun Caliphate</b>: &#8220;The first caliphs, successors to the Prophet Muhammad, turned a religious community into an empire reaching Syria, Egypt and Iraq.&#8221; Find one detail on its page that supports this, and one question it leaves open.</p><span class="ws-lines" aria-hidden="true"><span></span><span></span><span></span></span></li><li><p>Stop 9, <b>Mughal Empire</b>: &#8220;Akbar's Sulh-i-kul, or 'peace with all', and his abolition of the jizya in 1564 contrast with Aurangzeb's return to orthodoxy.&#8221; How does this last stop connect to an earlier one?</p><span class="ws-lines" aria-hidden="true"><span></span><span></span><span></span></span></li><li><p>The path sums itself up as: &#8220;How rulers adopted, promoted and imposed faiths, and how faith in turn shaped their states.&#8221; Which stop shows this best? Explain your choice.</p><span class="ws-lines" aria-hidden="true"><span></span><span></span><span></span></span></li></ol></section>
<p class="ws-src">From The Epoch Institute: the-epoch-institute.org/paths/religion-and-power. Free to print and copy for use in class.</p>
<details class="ws-key" id="answers"><summary>Answer key</summary><div class="ws-key-b"><p class="ws-key-t">Answer key: Worksheet: Religion and Power</p><section><h2><span class="ws-n">1</span>Put the stops in order</h2><ol class="ws-key-list"><li>Maurya Empire <span>from 322 BCE</span></li><li>Aksumite Empire <span>from 100 CE</span></li><li>Edict of Milan <span>313 CE</span></li><li>Rashidun Caliphate <span>from 632 CE</span></li><li>Coronation of Charlemagne <span>800 CE</span></li><li>Safavid Empire <span>from 1501 CE</span></li></ol></section><section><h2><span class="ws-n">2</span>Think and discuss</h2><p class="ws-key-note">Open questions: answers will vary. Good answers use details from the pages of the path.</p></section></div></details>
